WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.136
What a working engineer sets before the first real run.

2
00:00:03.416 --> 00:00:09.176
Before an agent touches real work, a careful engineer writes down five numbers and three rules.

3
00:00:09.456 --> 00:00:21.808
The numbers: maximum model calls, maximum tokens (or dollars) per task, maximum wall-clock time, maximum size of any single tool result, and the retry count for transport errors.

4
00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:30.131
The rules: what counts as done and how it is verified, what counts as no progress, and what the agent returns when it is stopped early.

5
00:00:30.411 --> 00:00:39.691
Then they run a small batch of representative tasks with generous limits, look at the traces, and set the limits from the distribution they observe, with headroom.

6
00:00:39.971 --> 00:00:44.771
That is the same evidence-first habit as Studio 3, applied to the loop itself.

7
00:00:45.051 --> 00:00:47.931
They also decide who reads the stop reasons.

8
00:00:48.211 --> 00:01:02.483
A weekly look at how runs ended (done, capped, stuck, refused) is the cheapest early warning you will get: a rising share of capped or stuck runs usually means a tool, a prompt, or a model version changed underneath you.
